 "stem": "If $f(x)=x^2-{=p}x+{=q}$ and $g(x)=x-{=r}$, find $f(\\sqrt{{=n}})+{=p}\\,g(\\sqrt{{=n}})$.",
 "answer": "n + q - p * r",
 "solution": [
  "$f(\\sqrt{{=n}})=(\\sqrt{{=n}})^2-{=p}\\sqrt{{=n}}+{=q}={=n+q}-{=p}\\sqrt{{=n}}$.",
  "$g(\\sqrt{{=n}})=\\sqrt{{=n}}-{=r}$, so ${=p}\\,g(\\sqrt{{=n}})={=p}\\sqrt{{=n}}-{=p*r}$.",
  "Add: $f(\\sqrt{{=n}})+{=p}\\,g(\\sqrt{{=n}})={=n+q}-{=p}\\sqrt{{=n}}+{=p}\\sqrt{{=n}}-{=p*r}$.",
  "The surd terms cancel, so the sum is ${=n+q-p*r}$."
 ],
 "proposed_misconceptions": [],
 "notes": "Mirrors book drill: coefficient of g equals the x coefficient in f so the surds cancel; answer is a whole number (may be negative)."
